## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Chemicals
All chemicals were analytical grade and used without further purification. 2-Amino-benzenedicarboxylic acid ( $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC) was purchased from TCI (Shanghai, China). Acid phosphatase (ACP), glucose oxidase (GOx), human serum albumin (HSA), and immunoglobulin G (IgG) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (Shanghai, China). Serine (Ser), alanine (Ala), histidine (His), arginine (Arg), aspartic acid (Asp), cysteine (Cys) and tryptophan (Try) were purchased from Shanghai Kangda Biotechnology Co. Ltd. (Shanghai, China). Glucose (Glu) and fructose (Fru) were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co. (Shanghai, China). N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF), ferric chloride hexahydrate $\mathrm{(FeCl_{3}{\cdot}6H_{2}O)}$ ), OPD, and sodium pyrophosphate were supplied by Chongqing Taixin Chemical Co. Ltd. (Chongqing, China). Human serum samples were obtained from a local hospital (the Ninth Hospital of Chongqing, Beibei). The stock solution of OPD was freshly prepared daily with ethanol, and the other solutions were prepared using ultra-pure water.
## 2.2. Apparatus
Scanning electron microscope (SEM) images were acquired on a Hitachi S-4800 field emission electron microscope (Hitachi, Japan), operated at an accelerating voltage of $25~\mathrm{kV}.$ X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the MOFs were collected by using a TD-3500 X-ray diffractometer (Dandong, China), equipped with a nickel-filtered 0.15406-nm Cu Kα source operated at $20\ \mathrm{mA}$ and $30~\mathrm{kV}.$ . Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra were acquired with a Tenson 27 FT-IR spectrometer (Bruker, Germany). Zeta potentials were measured with a Malvern Instruments Zetasizer Nano-ZS (Malvern, UK). UV-vis absorption spectra were acquired on a UV-2450 spectrophotometer (Shimadzu, Japan). Fluorescence spectra were acquired with a Hitachi F-2700 spectrophotometer (Hitachi, Japan).
## 2.3. Synthesis of $N H_{2}$ -MIL-101 MOFs
MOFs were prepared as previously reported (Bauer et al., 2008; Kathryn et al., 2009). Briefly, the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101 was synthesized by hydrothermal treatment of a 2:1:156 molar mixture of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC, and DMF at $110~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . MIL-101 was prepared similarly, except that $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC was replaced by BDC. The MOFs were collected by centrifugation, washed with DMF and ethanol several times, and dried thoroughly at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
2.4. OPD oxidation by $H_{2}O_{2}$ in the presence of $N H_{2}$ -MIL-101 or MIL-101
Initially, $0.1~\mathrm{mL}$ of $250~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101 or MIL-101 was added to $4.4~\mathrm{mL}$ of acetate buffer (0.2 M, pH 4.0). Then, $0.25\mathrm{mL}$ of $3\mathrm{mMH}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $0.25\mathrm{mL}$ of $0.1\mathbf{M}$ OPD were introduced. After an incubation of the mixtures at $37\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20\ \mathrm{min}$ , 430–650 nm fluorescence spectra ( $410~\mathrm{nm}$ excitation) were recorded. The excitation and emission slit widths were $5\mathrm{nm}$ and $10\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively.
## 2.5. Detection of PPi and ACP
A series of PPi solutions with different concentrations over the range $0{-}25~\upmu\mathbf{M}$ were added to $0.1\mathrm{mL}$ of $250~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}/{\mathrm{mLNH}}_{2}$ -MIL-101, $0.25\mathrm{mL}$ of $3\mathrm{mMH}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , and 0.25 mL of 0.1 M OPD in acetate buffer (0.2 M, pH 4.0). After incubation at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 20 min, 430–650 nm fluorescence spectra at $410~\mathrm{nm}$ excitation were recorded. ACP at different concentrations ranging over $0{\mathrm{-}}100~{\mathrm{U/L}}$ was mixed with $10~\upmu\mathbf{M}$ PPi in $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of acetate buffer (10 mM, pH 5.0). After incubation at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ , the mixture was added to 0.1 mL of $250~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101, $0.25~\mathrm{mL}$ of $3\mathrm{mM}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , and 0.25 mL of $0.1\mathbf{M}$ OPD in acetate buffer (0.2 M, pH 4.0). These solutions were then treated as described above for PPi.
## 2.6. Assay for ACP in human serum samples
Human serum samples were treated in 30-kDa Amicon ultrafiltration cells at
$3000\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . Then, the filtrate was diluted 100 times with acetate buffer for ACP detection in the serum, as described above.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of $N H_{2}$ -MIL-101
The SEM image in Fig. 1A indicates that the synthesized $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101 had an octahedral morphology with a diameter around $400~\mathrm{nm}$ . The XRD pattern in Fig. 1B indicated that the characteristic peaks of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101 were consistent with a simulation of MIL-101; a pure MOF phase was indicated by the absence of other peaks. Based on the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101 chemical structure (Bauer et al., 2008; Stavitski et al., 2011) (Scheme S1), the $770~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ peak in the FT-IR spectrum (Fig. 1C), was attributed to C-H bending vibrations in benzene (Li et al., 2017). The peaks at 1431 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1578~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ were asymmetric and symmetric vibrations of carboxyl groups in the frameworks (Wu et al., 2013). The 1656 cm peak for $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ stretching was from guest DMF CONH bands (Barbosa et al., 2017). Peaks at $3462~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $3372~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ were attributed to asymmetrical and symmetrical amine stretching (Zhang et al., 2016). The fluorescence spectrum of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101 (Fig. 1D) exhibits an intense fluorescence emission at $456~\mathrm{nm}$ , which originated from its organic linker $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC (Fig. S1D). To verify this, MIL-101 was prepared under the same conditions as $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101, except that $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC was replaced with BDC. The MIL-101 exhibited a similar morphology (Fig. S1A) and similar crystal diffraction (Fig. S1B) as $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101, but no fluorescence emission at $456\mathrm{nm}$ was observed (Fig. S1C).
