## 3.3. Sensing principle of PPi and ACP via ratiometric fluorescence
PPi readily forms complexes with metal ions. Hence, when it was introduced into the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL- $101/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}/$ OPD system, it coordinated with the Fe-O cluster in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101, inhibiting the catalytic activity on OPD. As shown in Fig. 3A, the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101/H2O2/OPD system exhibited increased the $F_{456}$ intensity with increasing PPi concentration, while the $F_{556}$ intensity decreased. The PPi-induced decline in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101 catalytic activity was confirmed by kinetics shown in Fig. 3B, which plots $F_{556}/F_{456}\mathrm{vs}$ . reaction time at different PPi concentrations. The slopes decreased with increasing PPi concentration, suggesting that PPi inhibited the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101 catalytic activity.
Because ACP catalyzed the hydrolysis of PPi, the catalytic activity of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101 could be recovered by adding ACP to the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101/H2O2/OPD/PPi system. As shown in Fig. 3A, the DAP fluorescence at $556\mathrm{nm}$ was restored in the presence of ACP, while the fluorescence of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101 at $456\ \mathrm{nm}$ was quenched. PPi analogues, such as $\mathrm{PO_{4}}^{3-},\mathrm{~HPO_{4}}^{2}$ -, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}\overline{{}}$ , had negligible effects on both the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101/OPD catalysis (Fig. S5A), and the inhibitory behavior of PPi toward $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101 (Fig. S5B).
