## 4. Conclusion
In conclusion, a ratiometric fluorescence sensor for PPi and ACP, based on bifunctional $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101 MOFs, was demonstrated. As a peroxidase mimic catalyst, the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101 accelerated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ oxidization of OPD to produce fluorescent DAP, which then quenched $F_{456}$ emission from the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101 MOFs. The inner filter effect between $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101 MOFs and DAP was mainly responsible for the quenching. Because of its specific binding to Fe centers in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101, the
PPi-induced decrease in the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101 catalytic activity in OPD oxidation, and the specific hydrolysis of PPi by ACP, the ratiometric fluorescence enabled sensitive and selective detection of ACP activity. The ratiometric sensor exhibited a wide linear range (0.01−30 U/L), high sensitivity $_{\mathrm{~\tiny~\leftmoon~DD=0.005~I}}$ U/L), and good selectivity for ACP detection. The protocol was used to successfully analyze ACP activity in spiked biological samples of human serum. PPi/ACP-mediated fluorescence changes in the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101/H2O2/OPD system also enabled a functioning cascade INHIBIT logic gate. It is anticipated that such a new strategy based on luminescence and biomimetic catalytic activity of bifunctional LMOFs could enable promising applications in biological analysis and clinical diagnostics.
